  "thought": "Reviewing the updated canonical AX Code Wasm diff API for Desktop integration. This is a read-only advisory assessment.\n\nThe review targets commit 912c8f92c4d66d0ad8a2887251c3325bbf8b5718. Desktop already uses Pierre hunk metadata for rendering, navigation, revert and Git staging. The tested Markdown CRLF fix is a block-only lex optimization, not a Wasm migration. No benchmark results will be invented.\n\nThe review will flag concrete feasibility issues in API-to-UI mapping, subset and reverse apply correctness, publication packaging, and measurements required before replacing Pierre. It will also determine whether a dependency-ready prototype can be built within current repository rules or if canonical package delivery is mandatory first.\n\nThe package is private and unlisted. It depends on `@ax-code/util` via workspace. Exports are browser and Node entry points. Generated `dist/` and `wasm/` folders are gitignored and must be deployed with a NOTICE file. The build requires rustup wasm target, wasm-bindgen-cli 0.2.129, and native diff compilation. No fallback exists inside the API. It runs in a single worker with one in-flight call and no queue. Cancellation terminates the worker.\n\nThe API enforces hard limits: 16 MiB text, 4096 hunks, and 200k lines. The `diff` function returns one-based structured hunks with four context lines and empty ranges. Applying a selected subset requires rebasing its `newStart` coordinates. Normalized diff output uses LF-normalized hunks but raw addition and deletion counts; `applyHunks` must target an explicitly LF-normalized source. A separate `applyPatch` API mirrors the native patch worker and returns computed text without file I/O. Browser deployment uses module workers, Wasm CSP, and application/wasm MIME; COOP/COEP headers are unnecessary.\n\nAX Wiki export CSP remains unchanged. The browser diff engine instantiates its worker via a module URL. UI packages must not depend on Electron, Express, node-pty, or simple-git. Desktop packages may only import sibling source through documented entrypoints or the public `@defai-digital/ax-code-sdk` exports. The workspace never resolves AX Code from sibling source paths or ignored build output.\n\nThe SDK is pinned to exact version 2.6.67 from GitHub Packages and must not be floated. The UI package is a shared React library, so bundling Wasm workers carries significant implications. Desktop currently uses Pierre's `parseDiffFromFile` via `getFileHunks`, which returns hunks containing `hunkSpecs` for display and accessibility.\n\nA potential shape mismatch exists between `additionLines` and `deletionLines`. The review list component renders them as numbers for display, but `revertHunk` treats them as arrays of strings that get joined. This inconsistency must be resolved before the UI can safely consume the canonical diff API.\n\nThe revert comment clarifies that trailing newlines live in the result of `diffAcceptRejectHunk`, not in the hunk metadata itself. This confirms the review list renders `additionLines` and `deletionLines` as numeric counts, while `revertHunk` joins string arrays. The two shapes must be reconciled before the UI can safely consume the canonical diff API.\n\nDesktop Git staging matches hunks by `candidate.newStart === hunk.additionStart`. Navigation derives anchors from the same Pierre parse. Rendering uses Pierre's parse directly.\n\nThe visual diff renderer is likely a separate Pierre component outside the pasted files. The user confirmed Desktop currently uses Pierre's hunk metadata consistently for rendering, navigation, revert and Git staging.\n\nA critical mapping issue has emerged. Wasm returns structured hunks with one-based coordinates, four context lines and empty ranges. Pierre's parseDiffFromFile segmentation, context size and header format are not specified to match these exact shapes. Hunk identity between the two APIs is therefore not guaranteed.\n\nGit staging matches hunks by `additionStart === newStart`. If Wasm context or splitting diverges from Pierre, stage buttons may attach to the wrong row. The UI already hides unmatched rows, so coverage drops rather than breaking.\n\nThe canonical preview shape is `{ version, additions, deletions, hunks }`. It does not expose `hunkSpecs`, `additionLines`, `deletionLines` or `additionStart`. These display fields are missing from the public API.\n\nThe missing `hunkSpecs` field means header strings and accessibility labels must be synthesized from the structured preview. Raw addition and deletion counts would also misalign with the displayed +N/-N badges if they are not normalized.\n\nRendering must remain Pierre. Replacing only `getFileHunks` while Pierre still paints the diff splits the single shared computed snapshot requirement. A prototype that computes Wasm hunks for the list but lets Pierre render would desync row identity from painted hunks.\n\nThe plan requires a single shared computed snapshot for display and hunk identity. Pierre's renderer parses internally and does not accept external hunk objects, so it cannot consume Wasm hunks directly. This means the preview cannot drive the on-screen diff; it must remain read-only beside Pierre. Either keep Pierre as the sole painter and use Wasm only for shadow comparison, or replace the painter entirely.\n\nA concrete feasibility bug is driving the review list from Wasm while Pierre paints. This splits hunk identity.\n\nThe existing `getFileHunks` is synchronous and cached. Wasm `diff()` is async and single-threaded. The UI would need a snapshot store keyed by owner and revision, plus cancellation and stale discard.\n\nMultiple files cannot share one Wasm engine without serialization. Each file needs its own worker and instantiation, so the 64-entry synchronous cache does not apply. Admission cannot be interrupted mid-encoding. The React component returns null when hunks are empty, which risks flashing blank lists during async fetches. Pierre parses synchronously and re-entrantly, but Wasm is not.\n\nThe current revert logic uses Pierre's `diffAcceptRejectHunk` to reject one hunk and join the remaining lines into a full file. Wasm's `applyHunks` applies hunks forward onto a source buffer, so true revert requires applying the complement of the selected hunk or generating a reverse patch.\n\nThe README states that applying a selected subset requires rebasing its `newStart` coordinates. This is necessary because omitting earlier hunks shifts the line numbers of the remaining ones. The documentation does not provide a rebase helper for this operation.\n\nDesktop must not implement its own rebase math. The canonical diff engine is the only kernel; building a second one violates the reuse requirement. Context checks in `applyHunks` catch obvious mismatches, but they cannot prevent silent mis-rebases when coordinates still align with the wrong context. Desktop needs a documented, tested `rebaseHunks` helper from the canonical API.\n\nReverse hunks are not a documented operation. `applyPatch` is a different model with fuzzy whitespace matching and smart punctuation, so it must not be mixed with strict `applyHunks`. Until the canonical API provides a tested rebase helper, subset apply remains unsafe to implement in Desktop.\n\n`applyHunks` preserves empty ranges and missing final newlines only when the hunks originate from the same engine and are applied to the matching source buffer. Desktop must not use `normalize: true` for reverting on-disk CRLF files, because counts must stay raw while coordinates remain LF; mixing normalization with raw disk bytes would desync Git staging, which relies on raw `git diff` `newStart`. The UI revert logic depends on `hunkIndex` into the Pierre array, so Wasm hunk order must be stable and identical to what is rendered.\n\nEmpty ranges can insert hunks that Pierre omits, shifting indexes and causing revert to target the wrong change. Git staging matches by `newStart === additionStart` and takes the first match; duplicate starts from empty ranges or adjacent hunks would bind the wrong hunk. Wasm's four-line context differs from Git's default three-line context, so boundary segmentation will diverge. Aligning by a single line number is already fragile, and changing the diff engine without updating the matcher makes it worse.\n\nStage migration is blocked until hunk boundaries are proven equal to Git's hunks, or the matcher adopts a stronger identity such as oldStart, oldCount, newStart, newCount, and header.\n\nRepository rules also restrict prototype packaging. Desktop packages may not import sibling source trees or build artifacts. The workspace must never resolve AX Code from a local sibling path. The SDK must be pinned to an exact version from GitHub Packages.\n\nThe new package is `@ax-code/diff-wasm`, private and not the SDK. It depends on `workspace:*` for `@ax-code/util`, which must be rewritten to a pinned published version or bundled. Its `private: true` flag prevents registry installation. The `dist/` and `wasm/` directories are gitignored, so no build artifact exists without the Rust toolchain, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129, and `pnpm build:native diff`.\n\nDesktop CI lacks the Rust toolchain and wasm-bindgen required to build this package, so Desktop would have to fork its own runtime. The UI package must not absorb a Wasm build chain; the browser worker URL must survive Vite bundling and the worker must fetch the `.wasm` asset. Desktop’s restrictive CSP (ADR-018) requires `wasm-unsafe-eval` for Wasm compilation, which the README already notes.\n\nADR-018 currently forbids unsafe-eval, so adding wasm-unsafe-eval requires an explicit security-baseline change rather than a silent CSP edit. The Electron file:// or custom protocol must serve the WASM module with the correct MIME type. Vite module workers require same-origin access to both the worker script and the WASM asset. COOP/COEP headers are unnecessary because SharedArrayBuffer is not used. Packaged applications must bundle the WASM asset inside the Electron asar or extraResources with the proper MIME type.\n\nThe package `files` field lists `dist`, `wasm`, and `NOTICE`, but those directories are gitignored, so a published tarball must be built in AX Code CI. Desktop must consume an exact-version registry tarball, not a range or `workspace:*`.\n\n`@ax-code/util` is flagged as a runtime dependency of the browser bundle. If it imports Node-specific paths, it would pull server-only builtins into the renderer. The UI must not depend on server packages, so the browser entry point needs verification that it does not import util's Node-oriented code.\n\nThe browser export `dist/browser.js` depends on `@ax-code/util`, so Desktop must either verify that the renderer tree-shakes to a browser-safe subset or bundle util into the Wasm package itself. This would let Desktop depend on a single package without traversing the workspace graph.\n\nThe adapted jsdiff code carries a BSD-3-Clause NOTICE. Desktop’s attribution file must include this notice when the dependency is imported.\n\nNo publication is authorized, so a dependency-ready prototype cannot be built inside the current repository rules. Integration requires the canonical package to be delivered first as a public or installable artifact with `private` removed or a separate publishable name. The `workspace:*` dependency on `@ax-code/util` must be replaced by an exact pinned published version or inlined into the artifact. The published tarball must contain `dist`, `wasm`, and `NOTICE`. Desktop must pin that exact version and avoid any sibling path.\n\nBefore replacing Pierre, the canonical engine must be validated against a frozen corpus of real review fixtures. Measurements are required for CRLF, no trailing newline, Unicode and emoji, empty files, binary skips, files over 16 MiB, overlapping edits, and files with many hunks. Hunk segmentation parity must be checked against Pierre and against `git diff -U3`, because Desktop staging matches against Git's own hunks.\n\nThe validation must report a mismatch rate rather than a single benchmark score. It should specifically measure the newStart equality rate used by the stage matcher and flag cases where duplicate newStart values create ambiguity.\n\nFor each hunk index, the canonical subset apply must be tested byte-for-byte against Pierre's `diffAcceptRejectHunk(..., \"reject\")`, including CRLF and missing final newline handling. Desktop cannot pass this test by writing its own rebase kernel, because that would validate the clone engine instead of the canonical one.\n\nThe apply-all roundtrip must be re-verified against Desktop's raw file snapshot bytes, not LF-normalized copies.\n\nHunk identity stability across worker recreations needs verification. The same inputs must yield identical hunk IDs.\n\nAsync UX timing is unknown. The worker cold-start delay, per-file diff latency, and N-file serialization under the one-in-flight constraint must be measured. It cannot be assumed faster than the current synchronous Pierre path.\n\nMemory costs include one worker per engine and the overhead of terminating workers on cancel.\n\nFailure modes must be handled explicitly. A stale owner or revision discard, an abort during encode, or a termination failure that blocks reuse are all risks.\n\nFiles that exceed the engine’s limits—16 MiB, 200k lines, or 4096 hunks—must keep Pierre’s existing too-large placeholder rather than returning empty hunks. The UI treats [] as “no hunks,” so a rejected diff cannot be encoded as an empty array.\n\nThe matcher must not switch to Git hunk indexes until parity is proven.\n\nThe revised plan is correct. Reuse the canonical engine rather than building another kernel. First deliver an independently installable pinned artifact with a public API. Then run a read-only worker preview against one shared snapshot. That preview must remain a parity harness or optional shadow, not the review list source, until the painter and actions share it. Keep Pierre’s actions in place until hunk semantics are proven identical. Only then should strict hunk reconstruction proceed with file snapshot checks.\n\nNo publication is authorized in this step. The Markdown CRLF fix stays separate from this review.\n\nThe subset apply path requires a rebase kernel that is not exported, so Desktop must not implement one.\n\nThe `createDuplicateComparator` alias is irrelevant to the UI path and should not expand scope.\n\nThe Electron renderer can use module workers directly. The web package’s Express server must serve the WASM asset with the correct MIME type for browser development. Both surfaces need coverage. Worker URL bundling is a Vite concern for the web package.\n\nThe UI package must not import `./node` paths. The API rejects concurrent requests, so the host must serialize diffs and cannot queue them unbounded. Rapid file switching would thrash workers by terminating them on cancel; that overhead must be measured.\n\nA single shared engine with a host-side queue is the correct shape. Terminating workers on cancel is a feasibility cost, not a reason to fork.\n\nSynthesizing aria labels from hunk coordinates is acceptable because git header matching is not used for identity today; identity depends only on newStart.\n\nThe Wasm engine includes empty ranges, while Pierre may omit pure context or empty hunks.\n\nIndex-based revert and focus via `data-review-hunk-index` would diverge from canonical hunk semantics. Filter rules must be derived from the canonical engine, not invented in Desktop.\n\nThe review will be structured as a verdict first, followed by API-to-UI mapping, subset and reverse apply, packaging, measurements, and prototype feasibility. It will be written in English per the user's explicit instruction, overriding the general Traditional Chinese rule for this turn.\n\nThe API-to-UI mapping reveals a critical rendering boundary. Pierre is synchronous and serves as the single source for paint, navigation anchors, revert, and Git stage matching. The Wasm `diff()` call is asynchronous, allows only one in-flight request per engine, and cancels kill the worker. Because `getFileHunks` runs inside `useMemo`, an empty array renders as no list. A rejected or in-flight Wasm call must therefore remain distinguishable from \"no hunks,\" or binary, oversized, or error files would disappear.\n\nThe preview snapshot must not supply Pierre's `hunkSpecs`. It fixes four lines of context and includes empty ranges, so it cannot drive navigation, revert, or Git stage matching. The read-only worker is a shadow keyed by owner and content hash; the host serializes requests, cancels on revision change, and discards stale results. The 16 MiB, 4,096 hunk, and 200,000 line bounds still require the existing too-large placeholder.\n\n`normalize: true` shifts coordinates but preserves raw line counts, so on-disk CRLF revert and Git `newStart` matching must still use the raw diff. Browser hosting requires a module worker, `application/wasm`, and an explicit Wasm compile permission in ADR-018’s script policy; the AX Wiki export CSP can remain unchanged.\n\nForward `applyHunks` is exact-context. Revert must restore one hunk while preserving all others, which requires applying the complement onto the before snapshot. The README mandates rebasing `newStart` for any subset, yet no rebase helper is exported. A Desktop rebase would be a second kernel and is out of bounds. Context checks fail closed on mismatch but cannot substitute for a canonical, tested rebase.\n\n`applyPatch` folds whitespace and punctuation, so it cannot serve as a revert path. Empty ranges shift hunk indexes relative to Pierre, making index identity unsafe until segmentation matches exactly. The only documented exact apply is the full hunk list, including a missing final newline. Subset revert must remain on Pierre until the canonical package can reconstruct one rejected hunk and tests match Pierre's bytes on CRLF, no trailing newline, Unicode, and empty ranges.\n\nThe package is marked `private: true` and depends on `workspace:*`, so no installable artifact exists yet. Desktop cannot import the local kernel, ignored build output, or a vendored copy. The browser entry must not pull Node-only utilities into the renderer; it should inline a browser-safe version or pin an exact published utility. Delivery requires a registry tarball containing `dist/`, `wasm/`, and `NOTICE`, with the existing jsdiff BSD notice carried into Desktop's NOTICE in the same change.\n\nDesktop CI must not adopt a Rust or wasm-bindgen toolchain to build the canonical package.\n\nA frozen corpus of edge cases should be tested first: CRLF, missing final newline, emoji, empty sides, binaries, oversize files, and many hunks. The canonical engine must be checked against Pierre and `git diff` for hunk-boundary equality, `newStart` match rate, and duplicate collisions. Byte equality of a single-hunk revert must match `diffAcceptRejectHunk`. Full apply round-trips on Desktop snapshot bytes must also pass. Worker cold start, per-file latency under one-in-flight load, and packaged CSP instantiation need measurement. Only after these checks pass should the painter and actions be replaced.\n\nThe first step is an owner-published, independently installable artifact with an exact pinned version. Its public API must expose subset rebase or single-hunk reverse reconstruction. A read-only shadow worker remains separate from the action path. The draft is roughly 750 words and will be tightened to stay safely under 800.\n\nThe canonical engine binds hunks by matching `newStart` to `additionStart`; duplicate coordinates would mis-bind navigation. The rendered addition count is a single number, not an array, because the JSX renders it as `+{hunk.additionLines}`; the revert path uses `resolved.additionLines` from `diffAcceptRejectHunk`, which is separate from the hunk object.\n\nThe canonical engine uses four context lines by default, while `git diff` defaults to three. This segmentation mismatch risks divergent hunk boundaries and shifted `newStart` coordinates whenever changes are four to seven lines apart. Farther changes may still align, but the boundary risk is real.\n\nWhen the canonical engine merges two changes that `git diff` would keep separate, the UI hides the stage button. That is safe but incomplete. A shared `newStart` across merged and unmerged hunks remains the dangerous collision case. The `createClient` worker snippet is browser-only; a Node export exists separately.\n\nThe Electron renderer must import the browser entry, not the Node worker, to avoid sandbox violations from Node filesystem or WASM paths. The host retains file snapshots and Git state, which aligns with the plan’s snapshot checks.",
